["Implementing such a system would require a deep understanding of LTE Downlink Control Information (DCI) messages and their structure. Here's a rough outline of how to start developing this type of system in C, but please note that this is just a basic skeleton. Further functionalities and error checks are not covered here due to the complexity and length of such tasks.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    /* Add the required physical layer reception indications (DL Layer Indication) 
       and demodulation reference signal (DMRS) configuration information here */
} DCIMessage;

typedef struct Node {
    DCIMessage* dciMessage;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL; // global variable representing the start of list 
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; // to manage concurrency and ensure data consistency.

void initialize() {
    head = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if(head == NULL) {
        exit(-1);  /* when there is not enough memory */
    }
    head->dciMessage = NULL; // no dci message initially.
    head->next = NULL;   // list initially empty.
}

void addNode(DCIMessage* msg) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);  // lock to ensure atomicity while updating the shared data structure

    Node* newNode = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
    if(newNode == NULL) {
        exit(-1); /* when there is not enough memory */
    }
    newNode->dciMessage = msg;
    newNode->next = head; // add at the start of list for O(1) complexity
    head = newNode; 

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlocking after updating
}

void deleteNode(DCIMessage* msg) { /* Note: Deletion logic may vary based on requirements */
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    Node* temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL && temp->dciMessage != msg) {
        prev = temp;
        temp = temp->next;
    } 

    /* now 'prev' is the node before the node to be deleted and 'temp' is the node to be deleted. */
    if(temp == NULL) {
       exit(-1); // no such DCIMessage found in list
    } else {
        prev->next = temp->next; /* bypassing 'node' */
        free(temp);  /* free allocated memory*/
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Similarly you can implement searchNode and iterateNodes functions.  
```
This is just a basic starting point. You would need to expand this code according to your requirements, add necessary error checks and handle edge cases such as memory allocation failures, null pointers etc., write additional methods for logging and performance monitoring, and ensure proper memory management (freeing of allocated memory when not required). Also, it's important to note that the design choices made here were based on the specific requirements mentioned. Depending upon the use case, other data structures or algorithms could be more suitable. 

