#define MAX_POOL_SIZE 1024 * 1024 // example max pool size

typedef struct {
    int key;
    long offset;
} Record;

Record* records[MAX_POOL_SIZE];
int recordCount = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void* allocate(size_t size) {
    void *ptr = NULL;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    if (recordCount + size > MAX_POOL_SIZE) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Out of memory.\n");
        exit(-1);
    } else {
        ptr = &records[recordCount];
        recordCount += size;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);

    return ptr;
}

void deallocate(void *ptr) {
    // For simplicity, we do not actually free memory in this example. 
    // In a real-world scenario, you would need to implement a custom memory pool and cleanup mechanism.
}

Record* create_record() {
    Record* record = allocate(sizeof(Record));

    if (record != NULL) {
        record->key = 0; // set default values
        record->offset = 0;
    }

    return record;
}

void destroy_record(Record *record) {
    deallocate(record);
}
```
This code creates a memory pool with a fixed size. When `allocate()` is called, it first locks the mutex to prevent other threads from modifying shared state while allocating memory. If there's enough space in the pool, `allocate()` returns the next available block and increments the record count. Otherwise, it prints an error message and exits. 

The functions `create_record()` and `destroy_record()` represent typical use cases for allocating and deallocating memory in a custom memory pool. They simply call `allocate()` or `deallocate()` respectively, setting default values for the record fields as necessary.

For concurrent access, this code uses a simple mutex lock to prevent data races on shared state. This is sufficient for many use cases, but may need to be adjusted based on your specific needs and constraints. 

