['Your task requires proficient knowledge of Java concurrency, multi-threading, exception handling, and logging. Here's a rough sketch of what this might look like:

```java
import java.security.*;
import javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class HashService {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(HashService.class);

    // Adjust according to system capacity and requirements 
    private ExecutorService executorService =  Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors() * 2);

    public String getConcatenatedMD5(List<String> strings) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        if (strings == null || strings.isEmpty()) {
            return "";
        }

        List<Future<byte[]>> futures = new ArrayList<>();
        for (final String str : strings) {
            Future<byte[]> future = executorService.submit(() -> 
                this.getMD5(str));
            futures.add(future);
        }

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (Future<byte[]> future : futures) {
            byte[] hashBytes = retrieveWithRetry(future);
            if (hashBytes != null) {
                String hexHash = DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(hashBytes);
                sb.append(hexHash);
            }
        }

        return sb.toString();
    }

    private byte[] getMD5(String str) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        md.update(str.getBytes());
        return md.digest();
    }

    private byte[] retrieveWithRetry(Future<byte[]> future) 
            throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { // Retry mechanism
            try {
                long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                logger.info("Starting MD5 operation at {}", startTime);

                byte[] hashBytes = future.get();

                long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                logger.info("Completed MD5 operation at {}", endTime);
                return hashBytes;
            } catch (ExecutionException ex) {
                if(i == 2) throw ex; // Throw after three attempts
                Thread.sleep((long) Math.pow(2, i) * 1000); // Exponential back-off
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```
This is a simple example and it doesn't account for all the requirements. Please note that Java's thread pool size should be set based on system capacity, not just available processors as you might over-subscribe the CPU leading to context switching cost that could degrade performance. Also, this code assumes an external logging library like Logback or SLF4J is used for logging. 

It doesn't consider concurrency issues within a single string, which would be necessary if strings are being mutated in another thread. You might need to add synchronization blocks around the getMD5() method, depending on your use case. 

Asynchronous processing can introduce latency under high concurrency conditions due to thread scheduling overheads and contention for locks within the thread pool. It could be improved by using a work-stealing thread pool or asynchronous non-blocking I/O to better distribute tasks across threads. Memory usage can also be optimized, like reusing buffers instead of creating new ones each time, or avoiding unnecessary object creation. 

Lastly, Java's `MessageDigest` is not thread safe. If we were to make it thread-safe, we would need to consider concurrent modifications (like hash collisions) and handle these cases appropriately.
']

