["This task is quite complex and can't be fully covered in one code block due to its complexity. Below is an example of how you could start with the JavaScript solution:

```javascript
// This class represents a stream of sorted data.
class DataStream {
    constructor(source) {
        this._source = source;
        this._buffer = []; // store chunks in buffer
        this._offset = 0; // track current offset in buffer
        this._backpressureListeners = new Set();
    }

    async _fetchChunk() {
        const chunk = await this._source.getNextChunk();
        this._buffer.push(...chunk);
        if (this._buffer.length >= BACKPRESSURE_THRESHOLD) {
            for (let listener of this._backpressureListeners) {
                listener();
            }
        }
    }

    onBackpressure(listener) {
        this._backpressureListeners.add(listener);
    }

    removeListener(listener) {
        this._backpressureListeners.delete(listener);
    }

    getNext() {
        if (this._offset === this._buffer.length) {
            return this._fetchChunk().then(() => this.getNext());
        } else {
            const result = this._buffer[this._offset];
            this._offset++;
            return Promise.resolve(result);
        }
    }
}

class MergingUtility {
    constructor() {
        this._streams = []; // array of DataStream instances
    }

    addDataStream(dataStream) {
        this._streams.push(dataStream);
        dataStream.onBackpressure(() => this.merge());
    }

    async merge() {
        let promises = this._streams.map((stream, i) => stream.getNext().catch(err => null));
        let results = await Promise.all(promises);

        // Find the minimum value and its index among all fetched data
        let minIndex = -1;
        for (let i = 0; i < results.length; i++) {
            if (results[i] !== null && (minIndex === -1 || results[i].value < results[minIndex].value)) {
                minIndex = i;
            }
        }

        // If there's a valid minimum, output it and recurse
        if (minIndex !== -1) {
            this._streams[minIndex].getNext().then(() => this.merge());
        } else {
            throw new Error('No streams left');
        }
    }
}
``` 
In the above example, `DataStream` represents a single sorted data stream that fetches data in chunks, and `MergingUtility` merges multiple data streams. However, this is only one way to tackle this problem. A different approach could be taken based on the specific requirements of your project. For example, instead of using Promises for backpressure, you might choose to use callbacks or events. Similarly, depending on how data sources are managed and dynamically added, you may want to design a more robust solution than this simple array-based approach. This code does not include error handling or advanced features like real-time updates due to space constraints but can be expanded upon as needed.
"]

["Here's a basic implementation of how you might approach this task in JavaScript using MutationObserver API for handling frequent updates, WeakMap for efficient memory management, and Set to avoid duplicates:

```javascript
const traverseDOM = (selector) => {
    const resultSet = new Set();
    const observedNodes = new WeakMap();

    const mutationHandler = (mutationsList) => {
        for(let mutation of mutationsList) {
            if (mutation.type === 'childList') {
                // Handle added or removed nodes
                Array.from(mutation.addedNodes).forEach((node) => {
                    if (node.nodeType === 1 && node.matches(selector)) {
                        resultSet.add(node);
                    }

                    const childList = node.querySelectorAll(selector);
                    childList.forEach((childNode) => {
                      resultSet.delete(childNode);
                      observedNodes.set(childNode, null);
                    });
                });

                Array.from(mutation.removedNodes).forEach((node) => {
                    if (observedNodes.has(node)) {
                        observedNodes.delete(node);
                    } else {
                        resultSet.delete(node);
                    }
                });
            }
        }
    };

    const observer = new MutationObserver(mutationHandler);
    observer.observe(document, { childList: true, subtree: true });

    // Handle already existing matching elements at the time of observing
    Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(selector)).forEach((node) => {
        resultSet.add(node);
        observedNodes.set(node, null);
    });

    return Array.from(resultSet);
};
```
This code sets up a MutationObserver to observe changes in the document subtree, including added and removed nodes. It also handles disconnected nodes by checking the WeakMap `observedNodes` if a node was previously observed or not. If an already existing matching element is found at the time of observing, it's added into both the `resultSet` and `observedNodes`. 

For better handling of rapidly changing elements, you could add additional checks for state changes (e.g., attribute changes). However, note that this might not be as efficient due to browser-specific optimizations or restrictions on observing such events.

Please note: MutationObserver is an experimental technology and may not be supported in all browsers. Additionally, it has limitations when used with `subtree` option set to true and a large number of nodes are added or removed at once. Consider using other strategies for handling rapid updates if they might occur frequently. 

Remember that performance bottlenecks can appear anywhere in your code, including DOM traversal itself. Therefore, it's crucial to identify such areas and optimize where necessary.
"]

