['Here's an example of how you could implement this using PHP, MySQLi for database connection, GuzzleHttp client for external API calls, and a simple locking mechanism for concurrent updates:

```php
<?php
use GuzzleHttp\Client;

class UserService {
    private $dbConnectionPool = [];
    private $lockFilePath = '/tmp/update_user_password.lock';
    private $authApiUrl = 'https://external-auth-api.com/validatePasswordStrength';

    public function __construct() {
        // Initialize db connection pool
        $this->dbConnectionPool = [new \mysqli($host, $username, $password, $database)];
    }

    /**
     * Update user password
     */
    public function updatePassword(int $userId, string $newPassword): bool {
        // Use file-based lock to ensure atomicity and handle concurrent requests efficiently
        $lockFile = fopen($this->lockFilePath, 'w+');
        if (flock($lockFile, LOCK_EX)) { 
            $httpClient = new Client();
            try {
                // Validate password strength using external API
                $response = $httpClient->post($this->authApiUrl, [
                    'json' => ['password' => $newPassword]
                ]);

                if ($response->getStatusCode() !== 200) {
                    $this->logError('Failed to validate password strength');
                    return false;
                }
            } catch (\Exception $e) {
                $this->logError($e->getMessage());
                return false;
            }

            $dbConnection = $this->getDbConnection();
            if (!$dbConnection) {
                $this->logError('Failed to get DB connection');
                return false;
            }

            // Update user password in the database
            try {
                $stmt = $dbConnection->prepare("UPDATE Users SET password = ? WHERE id = ?");
                $hashedPassword = password_hash($newPassword, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);
                $stmt->bind_param('si', $hashedPassword, $userId);
                if (!$stmt->execute()) {
                    $this->logError('Failed to update user password');
                    return false;
                }
            } catch (\Exception $e) {
                $this->logError($e->getMessage());
                return false;
            } finally {
                $stmt->close();
            }

            $dbConnection->close();  // Return connection to the pool
            flock($lockFile, LOCK_UN); // Release lock
            fclose($lockFile);

            return true;
        } else {
            $this->logError('Failed to acquire file lock');
            return false;
        }
    }

    private function getDbConnection() {
        // Use a simple round robin load balancing strategy for db connection pool
        static $index = 0;
        $db = $this->dbConnectionPool[$index % count($this->dbConnectionPool)];
        $index++;

        return $db->ping() ? $db : null; // If a connection is dead, try next one
    }

    private function logError(string $message): void {
        error_log('[UpdateUserPassword] ' . $message);
    }
}
```
This code uses GuzzleHttp for external API calls and file-based lock to handle concurrent requests. The password strength is validated against an external authentication service, and the result of the operation (success or failure) is logged using PHP's `error_log` function. The database connections are managed in a round robin fashion.
']

["Given the complexity of this task, it's beyond the scope of this platform to provide a complete solution. However, I can guide you on how to begin with implementing some parts of your request. Here is an example of how you could start managing linked lists and dynamically allocating memory for states from a given FSA in C:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

// Struct representing each node in the list
typedef struct Node {
    int state;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* createList(int* states, int numStates) {
    // Error handling for NULL FSA pointer
    if (states == NULL || numStates <= 0) {
        return NULL;
    }

    Node *head = malloc(sizeof(Node));  // Allocating memory for the head node
    if (head == NULL) {               // Error handling for memory allocation failure
        free(head);                   // Freeing allocated memory before returning NULL
        return NULL;
    }

    Node *tail = head;                // Initialize tail pointer to point to head initially

    // Populating the list
    for (int i = 0; i < numStates; ++i) {
        tail->state = states[i];      // Assigning state from array to current node's state

        if (i < numStates - 1) {       // If it's not the last state, create a new node for next state
            tail->next = malloc(sizeof(Node));

            if (tail->next == NULL) { // Error handling for memory allocation failure
                freeList(head);       // Freeing allocated list before returning NULL
                return NULL;
            }
        } else {                     // For the last state, set next pointer to NULL
            tail->next = NULL;
        }

        tail = tail->next;             // Moving tail pointer to the newly created node
    }

    return head;                       // Returning the head of the list
}
```

This is just a skeleton and doesn't cover all the aspects you mentioned. Implementing these features would involve more complex designs including considerations for external data sources, system dependencies, performance constraints, integration issues, resource management, compression feature, serialization/deserialization etc. 

