["Here's a simple implementation of a modular async reduce function in JavaScript using Promises, which can be extended further for advanced features like handling network errors or backpressure. This example is quite rudimentary and would need to be adapted according to your specific requirements.

```javascript
const asyncReduce = (array, initialValue, callback, url) => {
    let accumulator = Promise.resolve(initialValue);

    // Batch network requests with 50ms delay for optimization
    const fetchBatchDelay = 50;

    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        array.reduce((accumulationPromise, currentNumber, index) => {
            if (index % 10 === 0 && url) { // Batch network requests
                setTimeout(async () => {
                    try {
                        const metadata = await fetch(url).then(response => response.json());
                        accumulator = accumulationPromise.then((accumulatedValue) => 
                            callback(accumulatedValue, currentNumber, metadata));
                    } catch (error) {
                        reject(error); // Handle network errors or timeouts
                    }
                }, fetchBatchDelay * Math.floor(index / 10)); // Delay batch requests
            } else {
                accumulator = accumulationPromise.then((accumulatedValue) => 
                    callback(accumulatedValue, currentNumber));
            }

            return accumulator;
        }, Promise.resolve());

    resolve(accumulator);
    });
};
```

This function processes an array asynchronously with a delay to batch network requests for optimization. It also handles potential errors and provides a mechanism to fetch metadata from an external service. This is just a simple example, real-world usage would need more comprehensive error handling, logging and potentially some sort of backpressure or rate limiting logic.
"]

['Here is a simple implementation of your request using cJSON library and sqlite3 for database operations. This code doesn't include all the functionalities you mentioned, but it gives you an idea about how to start implementing such functionality in C programming language:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "sqlite3.h"
#include "cJSON.h"

typedef struct Product {
    int id;
    char name[50];
    int quantity;
} Product;

int process_json(const char* filename, sqlite3* db) {
    FILE *fp = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open file\n");
        return -1;
    }

    fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_END);
    long numbytes = ftell(fp);
    fseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_SET);  

    char *buffer = (char*)calloc(numbytes + 1, sizeof(char));
    if(buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory\n");
        return -2;
    }

    fread(buffer, sizeof(char), numbytes, fp);
    fclose(fp);

    cJSON *root = cJSON_Parse(buffer);
    if (root == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to parse JSON\n");
        free(buffer);
        return -3;
    }

    int num_products = 0;
    Product prod;
    const cJSON *product = NULL;
    cJSON_ArrayForEach(product, root) {
        if(!cJSON_IsObject(product)) continue;

        cJSON *id = cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive(product, "id");
        cJSON *name = cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive(product, "name");
        cJSON *quantity = cJSON_GetObjectItemCaseSensitive(product, "quantity");

        if(!cJSON_IsNumber(id) || !cJSON_IsString(name) || !cJSON_IsNumber(quantity)) continue;

        prod.id = id->valueint;
        strncpy(prod.name, name->valuestring, sizeof(prod.name) - 1);
        prod.quantity = quantity->valueint;

        char *sql_insert = sqlite3_mprintf("INSERT INTO Products VALUES (%d, '%q', %d);",
                                            prod.id, prod.name, prod.quantity);

        if(sqlite3_exec(db, sql_insert, NULL, NULL, NULL) != SQLITE_OK) {
            printf("Failed to insert into database: %s\n", sqlite3_errmsg(db));
            sqlite3_free(sql_insert);
            continue;
        }

        sqlite3_free(sql_insert);
        num_products++;
    }

    cJSON_Delete(root);
    free(buffer);
    return num_products;
}
```
This is a basic example and might need adjustments according to your specific needs. Error handling, performance measurements etc are not included for brevity's sake. 

In order to compile this code you would also need cJSON library installed on your system as well as SQLite3 development package. Also don't forget to link against sqlite3 and pthread libraries during compilation using `-lsqlite3 -lpthread` flags. 

Please note that handling JSON in C can be a bit tricky due to lack of high-level abstractions, so you might need additional tools like cJSON library or other JSON parsers for C/C++. Also, this code assumes that your database is already set up and the table "Products" exists with columns "id", "name", and "quantity". 

This code also doesn't manage memory properly, but it serves as a basic example of how you might integrate JSON parsing, data storage in SQLite3 database, error handling etc. It is recommended to use tools like Valgrind for detecting memory leaks and improving your C programming skills for better performance and less errors.
