['Here is a basic example for your problem in C language using `fork()` and `execvp()` system calls. Note that this does not handle all constraints you've mentioned (like managing dependency issues, resource limits, performance optimization, etc). You would need to add error handling code for each of these cases:

```c
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <signal.h>

// Function to execute command in background
int run_in_background(char *argv[]) {
    pid_t pid;

    // Fork the process and handle errors
    if ((pid = fork()) < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "fork() failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        return 0;
    }

    // Child process executes command
    else if (pid == 0) {
        execvp(argv[0], argv);

        // If exec fails, print error message and exit child
        fprintf(stderr, "exec() failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
        _exit(1);  // Using _exit to avoid calling atexit handlers in child
    }

    // Parent process continues without waiting for the command to finish
    else {
        signal(SIGCHLD, SIG_IGN);   // Ignore SIGCHLD signals until handled by parent
        return 1;
    }
}
```
You would call this function like `run_in_background((char*[]){"/bin/ls", "-l", NULL});`. This will execute the command "/bin/ls -l" in background and immediately return from the parent process. The child's exit status is not reported back to the parent, though it can be handled using signals or `waitpid()`.

Please note that this function only provides a basic example and might need modifications to fully meet your requirements. It doesn't handle cases like command exceeding system limits, external dependencies failing, etc.
