['Here's an example solution for your problem. This is a simplified version and doesn't include all the detailed features you requested. The MD5 calculation could be slow on large inputs and not handle spikes in traffic well. Consider using some kind of message queue or load balancer to distribute the traffic. Caching mechanism has been simplified as caches can get very complex and may affect performance if not designed properly. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;

public class HashingService {
    private readonly ConcurrentDictionary<string, string> cache = new ConcurrentDictionary<string, string>(); 

    public string GetMD5Hash(string input) {
        if (cache.TryGetValue(input, out var hash)) return hash;

        using (var md5 = MD5.Create()) {
            byte[] inputBytes = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(input);
            byte[] hashBytes = md5.ComputeHash(inputBytes);

            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
            for (int i = 0; i < hashBytes.Length; i++) {
                sb.Append(hashBytes[i].ToString("X2"));
            }

            cache[input] = sb.ToString();
            return cache[input];
        }
    }
}
``` 
This code creates an instance of the MD5 hash function, hashes the input and converts it to a hexadecimal string for storage in cache. It also uses ConcurrentDictionary from System.Collections.Concurrent namespace which is designed for high performance scenarios and thread-safe access. The TryGetValue method will check if the value already exists in cache before computing the hash again.

In an actual application you would likely want to use a more robust solution, possibly involving a combination of distributed caching (Redis, Memcached etc.), load balancing and queuing systems. You also might want to consider hashing on chunks of data instead of full strings if performance is an issue. A proper architecture should also be designed for scaling up including use of cloud services and containerization. 

In terms of integrating with existing services, it will depend heavily on what those services are but generally you would need a way to get the input from the user (like from a web request), pass that through this service, then send the output somewhere else (again likely via a web request). This could involve using asynchronous programming for high-performance scenarios.
