['This task involves setting up an asynchronous Rust application that uses Tokio for parallel execution and logs detailed information about tasks to a file. 
Here is a rough sketch of how this can be done:

```rust
use std::{ffi::CString, fs::File, io::Write, process};
use tokio::runtime::current_thread;
use tokio::time::delay_for;
use serde::Deserialize; // for parsing command line arguments into a struct
extern crate serde_json; // for JSON deserialization

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
struct Args {
    tasks: Vec<Task>,
}

#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]
struct Task {
    name: String,
    delay_ms: u64, // ms to simulate latency
}

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let args: Args = serde_json::from_str(&std::env::args().nth(1).unwrap())?;

    current_thread::Builder::new().enable_all().build()?.block_on(async {
        let mut logfile = File::create("log.txt")?;

        for task in args.tasks {
            tokio::spawn(async move {
                // simulate latency using delay_for
                delay_for(std::time::Duration::from_millis(task.delay_ms)).await;

                writeln(&mut logfile, &format_args![
                    "Executed task '{}' after {} ms",
                    task.name, task.delay_ms
                ])?;
            });
        }

        Ok(())
    })
}

async fn writeln<W: Write>(writer: &mut W, s: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    writer.write_all(s.as_bytes())?;
    writer.write_all(b"\n")?;
    Ok(())
}
```
This code is a sketch and doesn't include error handling for all failure points, caching strategies or degrade gracefully upon service outage mechanism. 

You should add error handling mechanism to catch failures while performing tasks, implement cache strategy based on your requirements. For instance, you could use an in-memory database like Redis or a file system caching strategy depending on the requirements of your application.

To degrade gracefully upon service outage, you may want to introduce circuit breaker mechanism (like Hystrix for Java) to fail fast and prevent cascading failures downstream. 

Make sure to handle potential panics properly by placing each `tokio::spawn` in a separate scope that can handle its own panic via the `catch_unwind` function.
