['Below is a high-level overview of how one might implement such a system in Java:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.security.*;
import javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.*;

public class HashService {
    private final ConcurrentMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private static final ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());

    public Future<String> getHashAsync(final String input, final String algorithm) {
        return executor.submit(() -> getHashSync(input, algorithm));
    }

    public synchronized String getHashSync(final String input, final String algorithm) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        if (cache.containsKey(input)) {
            return cache.get(input);
        }

        byte[] digest = null;
        long startTime = System.nanoTime();
        switch (algorithm) {
            case "MD5":
                digest = DigestUtils.md5Hex(input).getBytes();
                break;
            case "SHA-256":
                digest = DigestUtils.sha256Hex(input).getBytes();
                break;
            case "SHA-512":
                digest = DigestUtils.sha512Hex(input).getBytes();
                break;
        }

        String hash = DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(digest);
        cache.put(input, hash);
        long endTime = System.nanoTime();
        logMetric("Compute Time", algorithm, (endTime - startTime));
        return hash;
    }

    public boolean validateHashRemote(final String input, final String expected) {
        // Call to external API for remote validation
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }

    public boolean validateHashLocal(final String input, final String expected) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException {
        return getHashSync(input, "MD5").equals(expected);  // Or any other default algorithm
    }

    private void logMetric(String metricName, String tag, long value) {
        // Log to metrics database for performance analysis
    }
}
```

The class uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` for caching hashes. The `getHashSync()` method checks the cache first before calculating and storing the hash. It uses Apache Commons Codec library for digest generation, which provides efficient methods to generate MD5, SHA-256, and SHA-512 digests in a single line of code.

The `getHashAsync()` method returns a Future object that allows the caller to get the hash value at a later time using `Future#get()`. This is an asynchronous execution model suitable for I/O-bound tasks. It also uses a `ExecutorService` with a fixed number of threads (equal to the number of available processors) for executing hash computations concurrently.

The class is synchronized to ensure thread safety and consistency when multiple nodes are accessing shared resources in a distributed system. The validation methods are stubbed out, you'll need to implement these as per your application requirements.

Please note that this is a simplified implementation. For actual production use, one would need additional error handling, logging, metrics collection and reporting, security considerations, etc. You would also want to consider using a dedicated library for hashing instead of Apache Commons Codec for better performance and security. 

For testing purposes, you may use tools like JUnit or TestNG for writing unit tests covering all edge cases, including large inputs.
