["This task can be a bit complex and it might not entirely fit into this text box, but I will provide an outline of how you could approach this problem using C++14's standard library for thread-safe concurrency control (atomic operations & mutexes), the `std::unordered_map` container for storing records in a hash table to efficiently manage insertion, updates and retrieval based on key values, and Boost.Serialization library to perform binary serialization/deserialization of our custom `record` objects.

Please note that this is just a basic outline and actual implementation might vary significantly depending upon the actual needs and requirements of your project:

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <mutex>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <fstream>
#include <boost/archive/binary_oarchive.hpp> 
#include <boost/archive/binary_iarchive.hpp>  
#include <boost/serialization/vector.hpp>   

// Define a simple record class
struct Record {
  int key;
  std::atomic<int> value;

  template<class Archive>
  void serialize(Archive & ar, const unsigned int version) {
      ar & key;
      ar & value;
  }
};

// Define a concurrent hash map class to manage record objects
class ConcurrentHashMap {
public:
  ConcurrentHashMap() {}

  void addRecord(int key, int val){
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mapMutex);
    records[key] = Record{key, val};
  }

  // Update existing record or create new one if doesn't exist
  void updateOrAddRecord(int key, int val){
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mapMutex);

    auto it = records.find(key);

    if (it != records.end()) {
      // Record exists - update its value
      it->second.value = val;
    } else {
      // No record with such key found - create new one
      addRecord(key, val);
    }
  }

  std::optional<int> getRecordValue(int key){
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mapMutex);

    auto it = records.find(key);

    if (it != records.end()) {
      // Record exists - return its value
      return it->second.value;
    } else {
      // No record with such key found
      return std::nullopt;
    }
  }

  void serializeToFile(const std::string& filename){
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mapMutex);

    std::ofstream file(filename);
    boost::archive::binary_oarchive oa(file);

    for (auto &rec : records) {
      oa << rec.second;
    }
  }

  void deserializeFromFile(const std::string& filename){
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mapMutex);

    std::ifstream file(filename);
    boost::archive::binary_iarchive ia(file);

    Record record;

    while (ia >> record) {
      updateOrAddRecord(record.key, record.value);
    }
  }

private:
  std::unordered_map<int, Record> records;
  std::mutex mapMutex;
};
```
In this code, we have a `ConcurrentHashMap` class that manages our `Record` objects in a hash table-like structure. It ensures thread safety by using mutex locks for every operation involving record addition/update or retrieval from the records hashmap, ensuring only one operation at a time can be performed on the map without causing data corruption.

The binary serialization and deserialization is also thread safe and implemented in `serializeToFile` and `deserializeFromFile` methods respectively. It uses Boost's Binary Archive library to perform efficient serialization/deserialization of our custom `Record` objects. 

Please note that handling memory management is taken care by STL containers and smart pointers in C++, so you don't need to do anything explicitly for memory management. You could use RAII principles (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) to automatically manage the lifetime of your resources with the help of constructors/destructors or `std::unique_ptr` or `std::shared_ptr` smart pointers in case of more complex cases where you need to have shared ownership.
"]

