["Here is a simplified version of the code in C using pthread mutexes to handle concurrency safely and dynamic memory allocation strategies to minimize fragmentation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct data_record {
    int key;
    float weight;
    long offset;
    struct data_record *next, *prev;
} data_record;

data_record* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;

// Function to create a new node in the list
data_record* createNode(int key, float weight, long offset) {
    data_record* newRecord = (data_record*)malloc(sizeof(data_record));
    if (!newRecord) return NULL;

    newRecord->key = key;
    newRecord->weight = weight;
    newRecord->offset = offset;
    newRecord->next = newRecord->prev = NULL;

    return newRecord;
}

// Function to add a node to the list at the end
void append(int key, float weight, long offset) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Locking before modifying the list

    data_record* newNode = createNode(key, weight, offset);
    if (!head) { // If list is empty, make this node as head
        head = newNode;
        head->next = head->prev = head;
    } else { // Else append at the end and update pointers
        data_record* last = head->prev;
        newNode->next = head;
        (head->prev)->next = (newNode);
        newNode->prev = last;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock after modification
}

// Function to update the offset of a specific node in list
void updateOffset(int key, long newOffset) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // Locking before modifying data

    data_record* temp = head;
    do {
        if (temp->key == key) {
            temp->offset = newOffset;
            break;
        }
        temp = temp->next;
    } while(temp != head); // Traverse the list until we find a match or come back to the starting point

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // Unlock after modification
}

// Function to free memory of each node in list
void destroyList() {
    while(head) {
        data_record* nextNode = head->next;
        free(head);
        head = nextNode;
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a basic demonstration and there are many aspects that could be improved, such as adding error checking to ensure all memory allocations succeed. The code also does not include the random number generation for 'weight', which would require additional library functions and could have its own performance considerations. 

Also, the updating offset operation has linear complexity in the worst case scenario (the node being at the end of list). A more efficient approach would be to maintain another structure mapping keys to nodes if frequent updates are necessary. It should also be noted that memory is not deallocated for simplicity; a full-featured implementation would need to manage this efficiently, likely by using a combination of explicit allocations and garbage collection.
"]

