2. Use `add(id, stream)` to add an asynchronous iterable stream (like a Node.js readable stream).
3. Call `merge()` which returns an async iterator that yields objects with the stream ID and data buffer.
4. To remove a stream use `remove(id)`.

This code provides basic functionality but does not include all of the features requested, like error handling or performance optimization. For example, it doesn't prioritize streams or handle network failures. Depending on your specific needs, you may need to add more advanced error handling and recovery mechanisms as well as additional logic for managing stream priorities.
