["Here's a basic code snippet which implements a simple connection pool with dynamic scaling. Keep in mind this is only a starting point and does not incorporate all mentioned features as it would take a lot of space to fully implement them. 

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class ConnectionPool {
    private static final int MIN_CONNECTIONS = 1;
    private static final int MAX_CONNECTIONS = 50;
    private static final long CHECK_INTERVAL = 300 * 1000L; // 5 minutes in milliseconds

    private BlockingQueue<Connection> pool = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(MAX_CONNECTIONS);
    private ScheduledExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();

    public ConnectionPool() {
        for (int i = 0; i < MIN_CONNECTIONS; i++) {
            pool.offer(createConnection());
        }

        executor.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> checkAndAdjustPoolSize(), CHECK_INTERVAL, CHECK_INTERVAL, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
    }

    public Connection getConnection() throws InterruptedException {
        return pool.take(); // This will block if no connection is available
    }

    private void checkAndAdjustPoolSize() {
        int currentPoolSize = pool.size();

        // Here you'd retrieve the load metrics and health indicators from your monitoring system
        double averageResponseTime = 0;
        double successRate = 0;
        int serviceLoadLimit = 10;

        if (averageResponseTime > 500 || successRate < 0.9) { // Poor performance, scale down
            while(pool.size() > MIN_CONNECTIONS && pool.peek().isIdle()) {
                pool.poll();
            }
        } else if (currentPoolSize < serviceLoadLimit) { // Good performance, scale up
            int sizeToAdd = Math.min(serviceLoadLimit - currentPoolSize, MAX_CONNECTIONS - currentPoolSize);

            for (int i = 0; i < sizeToAdd; i++) {
                pool.offer(createConnection());
            }
        }
    }

    private Connection createConnection() {
        // In reality, you would probably use some kind of factory or builder pattern here to create your connections
        return new Connection();
    }
}
```
This class uses a `BlockingQueue` to manage the pool of connections. When a connection is requested and no connection is available in the pool, it blocks until one becomes available. The `checkAndAdjustPoolSize()` method checks the service's load metrics and adjusts the pool size accordingly. It does not consider external notifications about service health changes, as this would require integrating with an external system and might require additional complexities.
"]

