["This is an advanced task with several components and considerations. The problem can't be solved with a simple function as described in the question. Instead, you need to divide it into separate sub-problems, like: creating a linked list, managing multi-threaded environment, handling external data sources, implementing command line interface etc. Also, this code will heavily depend on system specifics and the particular requirements of your project. 

For example, here is a simplified implementation of a thread-safe dynamic list in C with basic functionalities:

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <assert.h>

typedef struct Node {
    int value;
    struct Node *next;
} Node;

Node *head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void add(int new_value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    Node *new_node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
    assert(new_node != NULL);

    new_node->value = new_value;
    new_node->next = head;
    head = new_node;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

int lookup(int value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);

    int id = 0;
    for (Node *current = head; current != NULL; current = current->next, ++id) {
        if (current->value == value) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return id;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return -1;
}
```

This code maintains a linked list of integers. It's thread-safe, meaning that it can be safely modified by multiple threads at the same time without causing inconsistencies or data corruption. Note that this is just a simple example and doesn't handle all the requirements you described. Also note that actual implementation might require more complex solutions to address various issues like memory usage optimization, handling of varying DCI message sizes, real-time updates of DCI configurations, managing network latency, implementing command line interface etc.
