['Here is a C implementation of `update_filename_extension` that meets all your requirements:
```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>
#define MAX_LEN 255

void update_filename_extension(char* filename) {
    size_t len = strlen(filename);

    if (len > MAX_LEN - 4) { // ".log" length is always 4
        printf("Warning: Filename truncated due to exceeding max length\n");
        filename[MAX_LEN - 5] = '\0'; // Keep space for null-terminator and '.log' extension
    }

    char* dot = strrchr(filename, '.');
    if (dot && !strcmp(dot, ".log")) {
        *dot = '\0'; // Remove old extension
    }

    size_t vowels = 0;
    size_t digits = 0;

    for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
        char c = filename[i];

        if (isdigit(c)) {
            ++digits;
            continue;
        } 

        switch (tolower(c)) {
            case 'a':
            case 'e':
            case 'i':
            case 'o':
            case 'u':
                ++vowels;
                break;
            default:
                continue;
        }
    }

    size_t total = vowels + digits;

    char buffer[20]; // max length for int64 is 19 chars + 1 for null-terminator
    sprintf(buffer, "%zu", total);
    strcat(filename, buffer);
    strcat(filename, ".log");
}
```
Please note that this function modifies the input string in place. The `strrchr` and `tolower` functions are used to determine file extensions and count vowels/digits respectively. A warning is printed if the filename exceeds the maximum length limit, after which it is truncated to fit within the max length constraint.
']

