["This is a complex task that requires a deep understanding of C programming, multithreading, real-time system design, and software engineering best practices. The following skeleton code provides an outline for managing DCI messages in a thread-safe manner. However, the actual implementation would involve handling many more intricate details and edge cases:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct dci_message {
    int id;
    char *data;
    struct dci_message *next;
} DCI_MESSAGE, *PDCI_MESSAGE;

// Head of the list
PDCI_MESSAGE head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void initialize() {
    // Initialize mutex and list here.
}

void add(int id, char *data) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    // Add DCI message to the end of the list.
    PDCI_MESSAGE new_message = (PDCI_MESSAGE)malloc(sizeof(DCI_MESSAGE));
    new_message->id = id;
    new_message->data = data;
    new_message->next = NULL;

    if (head == NULL) {
        head = new_message;
    } else {
        PDCI_MESSAGE current = head;
        while (current->next != NULL) {
            current = current->next;
        }
        current->next = new_message;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

PDCI_MESSAGE lookup(int id) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    // Lookup DCI message by ID.
    PDCI_MESSAGE current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        if (current->id == id) {
            break;
        }
        current = current->next;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return current;
}

void delete(int id) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    // Delete DCI message by ID.
    PDCI_MESSAGE prev = NULL, current = head;

    while (current != NULL && current->id != id) {
        prev = current;
        current = current->next;
    }

    if (current == NULL) {
        return;
    }

    if (prev != NULL) {
        prev->next = current->next;
    } else {
        head = current->next;
    }

    free(current);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
This is just a basic skeleton of how you can start coding this task. In reality, each aspect mentioned would require separate modules and significant amounts of code. This includes error handling, real-time notifications, external integration with message queues, in-memory caching, horizontal scaling, load balancing algorithms, dynamic resource management, configuration management, serializing/deserializing DCI messages to/from disk, etc. 

A complete solution would be quite large and complex, thus it's better to tackle this task step by step and use well-tested libraries where possible. The implementation details could vary depending on the specific requirements of your system or application.
