['Here is a high-level example of how you might structure this Java application using Spring Boot and Apache Kafka for messaging system. 

For simplicity, I'm only showing the main classes and interfaces. You would have to create additional classes and files based on your specific requirements, like configuration classes, service implementations, and DAOs for managing data.

```java
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.util.*;

@Service
public class ServiceManager {
    private Map<String, ServiceInstance> serviceInstances = new HashMap<>();

    @KafkaListener(topics = "${kafka.topic}")
    public void handleMessage(ServiceEvent event) {
        switch (event.getType()) {
            case START:
                startServiceInstance(event);
                break;
            case STOP:
                stopServiceInstance(event);
                break;
            default:
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid service event type");
        }
    }

    private void startServiceInstance(ServiceEvent event) {
        String instanceId = event.getInstanceId();
        if (serviceInstances.containsKey(instanceId)) {
            ServiceInstance serviceInstance = serviceInstances.get(instanceId);
            serviceInstance.start();
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Service instance not found");
        }
    }

    private void stopServiceInstance(ServiceEvent event) {
        String instanceId = event.getInstanceId();
        if (serviceInstances.containsKey(instanceId)) {
            ServiceInstance serviceInstance = serviceInstances.get(instanceId);
            serviceInstance.stop();
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Service instance not found");
        }
    }

    public void addServiceInstance(String id, ServiceInstance service) {
        serviceInstances.put(id, service);
    }

    public void removeServiceInstance(String id) {
        if (serviceInstances.containsKey(id)) {
            serviceInstances.remove(id);
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Service instance not found");
        }
    }
}
```
The `ServiceManager` class listens for Kafka messages and starts or stops a specific `ServiceInstance` based on the message's content. 

Each service instance is started or stopped as needed, with retry logic implemented in each individual `ServiceInstance` implementation. The audit trail of all activities could be logged using Spring AOP or custom aspect for each operation.

