["Here's a simple PHP script using Laravel (a popular PHP framework) that interacts with an external API, supports environment variables, runtime changes without downtime, and implements caching for improved performance under high loads. Please note that this is just a simplified example and doesn't cover all the details you requested.

```php
<?php

namespace App\Services;

use GuzzleHttp\Client;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Cache;

class ApiService
{
    protected $client;

    public function __construct()
    {
        $this->client = new Client([
            'base_uri' => env('API_BASE_URL'),
        ]);
    }

    public function getData($endpoint)
    {
        // check if data is cached
        if (Cache::has($endpoint)) {
            $data = Cache::get($endpoint);
        } else {
            try {
                $response = $this->client->request('GET', $endpoint);
                $body = $response->getBody();
                // convert the body to an associative array
                $data = json_decode($body, true);
                // cache for 60 minutes
                Cache::put($endpoint, $data, now()->addMinutes(60));
            } catch (\Exception $e) {
                // log the exception or handle it as needed
                \Log::error('API request failed: ' . $e->getMessage());
                $data = [];
            }
        }

        return $data;
    }
}
```

This service class has a constructor that initializes a Guzzle client with the base URL specified by an environment variable. The `getData` method fetches data from a specific endpoint, caches it for 60 minutes using Laravel's Cache facade, and retrieves the cached version if available.

Remember to add the API_BASE_URL in your .env file along with other relevant configurations like cache driver, redis server details etc.
