['This task involves a lot of detailed design and consideration of many aspects like concurrency control, memory allocation, error handling, server communication, logging protocol integration etc., which are typically implemented in large complex systems rather than a single function as described by the task. Here is a basic outline of how you might structure such a system:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

// Define the record structure
typedef struct Record {
    int value;
    struct Record *next;
} Record;

// Global variables for locking and linked list management
Record* head = NULL;
pthread_mutex_t lock;
int size = 0;

// Non-linear distribution algorithm to generate values within a range
int nonLinearDistribution(int seed, int min, int max) {
    srand(seed);
    double y = (double)rand() / RAND_MAX; // Random number between 0 and 1
    return min + y * (max - min + 1);     // Scale the number to within range
}

// Function to insert a record into linked list, thread-safe
void insertRecord(int value) {
    Record* new_node = (Record*) malloc(sizeof(Record));
    if (new_node == NULL) {
        printf("Error: Memory allocation failed.\n");
        return;
    }

    new_node->value = nonLinearDistribution(time(NULL), 0, value); // Initialize record with a unique key
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    new_node->next = head;
    head = new_node;
    size++;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Function to delete a record from linked list, thread-safe
void deleteRecord(int value) {
    if (head == NULL) return; // List is empty

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    Record *temp = head, *prev = NULL;

    while (temp != NULL && temp->value != value) { 
        prev = temp; 
        temp = temp->next; 
    } 

    if (temp == NULL) return; // Value not found in list

    if(prev != NULL) prev->next = temp->next; // If record is head of list
    else head = temp->next; // Else change head to next node

    free(temp); // Free memory
    size--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Cleanup function to release all resources
void cleanup() {
    Record* current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        Record* temp = current;
        current = current->next;
        free(temp);
    }

    pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock);
}
```
Note: This code does not cover all the aspects mentioned in your task, such as the non-blocking socket communication for logging to a remote server, configuration handling, different logging protocols support, scalable design with increasing number of concurrent loggers, evaluating test coverage and development speed when designing test strategies etc. Also, error checking is minimal in this basic code. In real world scenarios, you would need to handle errors more robustly and use proper logging techniques instead of printf for debugging.
']

