['Here is a C code snippet implementing a circular doubly linked list with keys stored in an array. This program is not complete by itself; you would need additional functions for insertion, deletion, search etc. Also, error handling and thorough unit tests are left out here due to their complexity. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct Record {
    int value;
    int* key_ptr;
} Record;

typedef struct Node {
    Record record;
    struct Node* prev;
    struct Node* next;
} Node;

Node* head = NULL;
int* keys = NULL;  // Array to store keys
int num_nodes = 0;  // Number of nodes in the list

// Initialize linked list with 'n' nodes, each having a value within given range and key pointer.
void init(int n, int minValue, int maxValue) {
    srand(time(NULL));

    keys = (int*) malloc(sizeof(int) * n);  // Allocate memory for keys array
    if (keys == NULL) {
        printf("Error allocating memory for keys\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    Node* current = head;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        // Create new node and allocate memory
        Node* new_node = (Node*) malloc(sizeof(Node));
        if (new_node == NULL) {
            printf("Error allocating memory for a new node\n");
            exit(1);
        }

        // Initialize record in the new node with random value and key pointer
        new_node->record.value = rand() % (maxValue - minValue + 1) + minValue;
        keys[i] = i;  // Use index of key array as a key
        new_node->record.key_ptr = &keys[i];  

        if (current == NULL) {
            head = new_node;
        } else {
            current->next = new_node;
            new_node->prev = current;  // Set previous node for the new node
        }

        // Make the list circular by setting last node to point back to the first node
        if (i == n - 1) {
            new_node->next = head;  
            head->prev = new_node;
        }

        current = new_node;  // Move to next node
    }

    num_nodes = n;
}
```
The above code initializes a circular doubly linked list with 'n' nodes, each having a value within a given range and key pointer. It also allocates memory for the keys array dynamically, ensuring efficient memory usage and minimizing fragmentation. The use of `rand()` function ensures that each node contains a random integer value within the specified range.
']

["Implementing all these features in a single function would be very complex and difficult to maintain. Instead, we could divide this task into several separate functions for better understanding and easier debugging. Here is an example of how you might approach writing such a function:

```javascript
class DOMTraverser {
    constructor(selectorFunction) {
        this.selectorFunction = selectorFunction;
        // caching results and last result list to balance cache size and freshness
        this.cache = new Map(); 
        this.lastResult = [];
    }

    updateSelector(newSelectorFunction) {
        this.selectorFunction = newSelectorFunction;
    }

    async traverseNodeAsync(node, depthLimit = 0) {
        // base case: if node is null or document has been fully loaded
        if (!node || (depthLimit === 0 && node === document)) return [];

        let result = this.cache.get(node);
        if(!result) {
            result = Array.from(node.childNodes).filter((childNode) => this.selectorFunction(childNode));

            // Lazy evaluation: only traverse child nodes when needed
            const promises = Array.from(node.childNodes)
                .map((childNode) => this.traverseNodeAsync(childNode, depthLimit - 1));
            result = [...result, ...await Promise.all(promises).then(arrays => arrays.flat())];

            // Update cache with the new results
            this.cache.set(node, result);
        }

        return result;
    }

    async traverseTreeAsync(depthLimit = 0) {
        const root = document.documentElement;
        this.lastResult = await this.traverseNodeAsync(root, depthLimit);
        return this.lastResult;
    }
}
```
