['Implementing such a complex function involves several components of distributed systems, concurrency control, transaction management, logging mechanisms, error handling and messaging queues. Below is an overview of how you can implement this using Java with Spring framework for the repository pattern and other supporting libraries. 

```java
// Imports omitted

@Service
public class UserProfileService {

    @Autowired private UserRepository userRepo;
    @Autowired private BillingSystemClient billingClient;
    @Autowired private MessagingQueue queue;
    @Autowired private CacheManager cacheManager;

    // Assume that PriorityQueue is a class defined by you.
    private final ConcurrentPriorityQueue<UpdateJob> updateQueue = new ConcurrentPriorityQueue<>();

    @Transactional(rollbackFor=Exception.class)
    public void updateUserProfile(String userId, UserProfile profile, int priority) {

        // Validate input data against predefined rules
        validateUserData(userId, profile);

        // Add the job to the queue with its priority
        UpdateJob job = new UpdateJob(userId, profile, priority);
        updateQueue.add(job);
    }

    private void processUpdateJobs() {
        while (true) { 
            try {
                // Get a batch of jobs from the queue.
                List<UpdateJob> batch = updateQueue.pollBatch();

                for (UpdateJob job : batch) {
                    String userId = job.getUserId();
                    UserProfile profile = job.getProfile();

                    // Update the user's data in repository ensuring transactional context.
                    userRepo.save(userId, profile);

                    try {
                        // Adjust the user credits based on the type of profile change.
                        billingClient.adjustCredits(profile.getProfileType());

                        // Notify interested parties about changes to the user's profile through messaging queue.
                        queue.sendMessage("UserProfileUpdated", new UserUpdateNotification(userId, profile));

                    } catch (ExternalSystemException e) {
                        // Handle transient errors and retry a few times before failing the job.
                        int retries = job.incrementRetryCount();

                        if(retries > 3){ 
                            // Notify about failed job, but don't halt processing
                            queue.sendMessage("UserProfileUpdateFailed", userId);
                            continue; 
                        }
                    }

                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // Handle unexpected errors and fail the entire batch of jobs.
                for (UpdateJob job : batch) {
                    queue.sendMessage("UserProfileUpdateFailed", job.getUserId());
                }
            }
        }
    }

    public void startProcessing() {
        new Thread(this::processUpdateJobs).start();
    }
}
```
Please note that this is a simplified version of the function and does not include details such as logging mechanism, distributed transaction management, error handling or messaging queue integration. Also, this code assumes certain dependencies like `UserRepository`, `BillingSystemClient`, `MessagingQueue` and `CacheManager` to be defined in your Spring context for injection.

For detailed implementation you may need to handle many more aspects such as thread safety, concurrency control, transaction management, error handling, logging mechanism etc which would require significant amount of code and might not fit into this format.
