["Here is a sample solution in C. Please note that the implementation has been kept simple for clarity but with potential improvements such as error handling, edge case checking etc. Also, it's assumed that all memory allocations will succeed which may not be the case in real-world scenarios.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct record {
    int value;
    int *key;  // pointer to external keys array
    struct record *next;
    struct record *prev;
} Record;

Record* create_record(int num_records, int **keys, int key_array_size) {
    if (num_records == 0 || key_array_size < num_records) return NULL;  // edge case check

    Record *head = NULL, *tail = NULL;
    int *used_values = calloc(RAND_MAX, sizeof(int));  // to avoid duplicates

    for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
        Record* newNode = malloc(sizeof(Record));
        if (!newNode) return NULL; // allocation failed

        int randVal;
        do { 
            randVal = rand();
        } while (used_values[randVal]); // ensure no duplicates

        used_values[randVal] = 1;  // mark as used

        newNode->value = randVal;
        newNode->key = keys[i];   // point to external keys array

        if (!head) {
            head = newNode;
        } else {
            tail->next = newNode;
            newNode->prev = tail;
        }

        tail = newNode;
    }

    // creating a circular doubly linked list
    if (head) {
      head->prev = tail;
      tail->next = head;
    }

    free(used_values);  // deallocate used values array

    return head;  // returns the head of the circular doubly linked list
}
```

In this code, we first create a `used_values` array to track all generated random values and check for duplicates. If a duplicate is found, generate another one until an unused value is obtained. After creating each node, its previous pointer points to the tail (last created node). When the entire linked list has been created, point the head's prev to the tail and vice versa to form a circular doubly linked list. Finally, deallocate the `used_values` array as it is no longer needed.

This function should be called with the number of records you want to create, the pointer to your external keys array, and the size of this array. The function will then return a pointer to the head of the created linked list which can be used for further manipulation or iteration over the records.
