["Here is an example of how you might implement such a function in C, with appropriate error handling, thread safety, and efficient memory management. However, keep in mind that this is just one way to approach it and depending on your specific requirements and constraints, other solutions may be more suitable or optimal. 

In this code we are considering pthreads for managing thread safety and a naive fixed-size allocator as the custom memory pool.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    long offset;
} offset_t;

typedef struct {
    int key;
} key_t;

typedef struct {
    int value;
} record_t;

typedef struct {
    offset_t* offsets;
    key_t* keys;
    record_t* records;
    int status; // 0 if successful, -1 if failure
} opaque_data_t;

static pthread_mutex_t allocateLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
#define ALLOCATE_POOL_SIZE (2 << 30) // 2GB allocation pool size
static char* allocPool = NULL;
static char* allocCursor = NULL;

opaque_data_t* allocate_memory(long num_offsets, long num_keys, long num_records, int min_value, int max_value) {
    opaque_data_t *data = malloc(sizeof(opaque_data_t));
    data->status = 0;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&allocateLock); // locking for thread safety

    if (allocPool == NULL) {
        allocPool = calloc(1, ALLOCATE_POOL_SIZE);
        allocCursor = allocPool;
    }

    long poolAvailableSize = ((char*)allocPool + ALLOCATE_POOL_SIZE) - allocCursor;

    if (poolAvailableSize < num_offsets * sizeof(offset_t) + 
                           num_keys * sizeof(key_t) + 
                           num_records * sizeof(record_t)) {
        // Not enough space in pool, return error status
        data->status = -1;
    } else {
        data->offsets = (offset_t*)allocCursor; allocCursor += num_offsets * sizeof(offset_t);
        data->keys    = (key_t*)allocCursor;    allocCursor += num_keys * sizeof(key_t);
        data->records = (record_t*)allocCursor; 

        srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random number generator
        for (long i = 0; i < num_records; ++i) {
            data->records[i].value = rand() % (max_value - min_value + 1) + min_value;
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&allocateLock); // unlocking after allocation

    return data;
}
```
This function allocates memory from a fixed-size pool, which is initialized on the first call to `allocate_memory` and only reclaimed when the program exits. If the requested space exceeds the available space in the pool, an error status will be returned instead of attempting to grow it.
The random value generator inside the loop for initializing each record's value ensures that even if memory allocation fails, a default value is set to minimize disruptions to the overall system performance. 
Please note: This code does not deallocate memory in case of failure. This could be improved by keeping track of successful allocations and freeing all successful ones in case of a failure. However, this would complicate the function further and is beyond the scope of the original question. 
