['This task is quite complex and requires a detailed understanding of programming concepts like dynamic memory allocation, concurrency control, system calls, error handling etc. Here's the skeleton of how you can start with your implementation but please note that this code needs to be refined further according to your specific requirements.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

typedef struct record {
    int integer_field;
    char *status_flag;
    time_t timestamp;
} Record;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
Record* pool;
int offset = 0, size = 1024 * 1024; // Initial memory pool size in records

void allocate(int count) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (offset + count > size) {
        size *= 2;
        pool = realloc(pool, size * sizeof(Record));
        if (!pool) {
            perror("Reallocation failed");
            exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
        }
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void* worker(void* arg) {
    char *cmd = (char*)arg;
    pid_t child_pid = fork();

    if (child_pid == -1) {
        perror("Fork failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    // Child process executes the command
    if (child_pid == 0) {
        execl("/bin/sh", "sh", "-c", cmd, (char *) NULL);
        exit(errno ? errno : EXIT_FAILURE);
    } else { 
        int status;
        wait(&status); // Parent process waits for child to finish

        return (WIFEXITED(status) && WEXITSTATUS(status) == 0) ? 1 : 0; // Return success or failure based on child's exit code
    }
}

int main() {
    srand(time(NULL));

    pool = malloc(size * sizeof(Record));
    if (!pool) {
        perror("Memory allocation failed");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 10000000; ++i) { // Example of 10M records
        allocate(5); // Allocates memory for five records at once

        for (int j = 0; j < 5; ++j, ++offset) {
            pool[offset].integer_field = rand();
            pool[offset].status_flag = malloc(1); // Initialize status flags
            *pool[offset].status_flag = 'A';
            pool[offset].timestamp = time(NULL);
        }
    }

    char cmd[] = "ls -l"; // Example command to execute in background
    pthread_t thread;
    int result;

    if (pthread_create(&thread, NULL, worker, cmd)) {
        perror("Thread creation failed");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    // Wait for the child process to finish and get its exit status
    pthread_join(thread, &result); 

    free(pool); // Free all allocated memory

    return result ? EXIT_SUCCESS : EXIT_FAILURE;
}
```
In this code snippet, the `allocate` function is used to manage memory allocation. It doubles the size of the pool whenever it's not enough for allocating new records and then reserves memory using realloc. Note that a lock mechanism is implemented here to ensure thread safety when allocating memory concurrently. 

The main process also runs a background process using a fork-exec-wait pattern. The command to execute in the child process is passed as argument to `worker` function, which is run in a separate thread. Its exit status will be returned by `pthread_join` at the end of the main process. 

Remember that you need to include your own memory management routines for the status flags and cleanup code when done with the pool. Also, the memory allocation metrics are not handled here, but you can add a profiling mechanism using the system's clock functions or other methods. 

Please note that the above code is a basic demonstration of how you could implement such functionality in C, and it may require substantial modification to suit your particular needs and environment. It doesn't include error checking for every possible failure condition; adding such checks would make the code quite lengthy.
