['Here's a simple implementation in C using dynamic memory allocation and linked list structure. This code assumes that you have a function `getRandomNumber(int low, int high)` to generate random numbers within the given range. You may need to replace this with actual implementation based on your requirements. Also note that error handling is kept very basic here:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct record {
    long offset;
    int key;
    int value;
    struct record* prev;
    struct record* next;
} Record;

Record* createRecords(int size, int low, int high) {
    if (size <= 0) return NULL;

    // Allocate memory for all records
    Record* head = malloc(size * sizeof(Record));
    if (!head) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    // Initialize first record
    head->offset = 0;
    head->key = 0;
    head->value = getRandomNumber(low, high);
    head->prev = NULL;
    head->next = NULL;

    Record* current = head;

    // Initialize rest of the records
    for (int i = 1; i < size; ++i) {
        current->next = &current[1];
        current->next->offset = current->offset + 1;
        current->next->key = i;
        current->next->value = getRandomNumber(low, high);
        current->next->prev = current;

        // Make the list circular
        if (i == size - 1) {
            current->next->next = head;
            head->prev = current->next;
        } else {
            current->next->next = NULL;
        }

        // Move to the next record
        current = current->next;
    }

    return head;
}
```
This code will create a circular, doubly linked list of 'record' structures with random values. Please note that this is a very simplified and abstract implementation for your problem statement, it doesn't include handling for multiple data sources or error handling in all scenarios. This would need additional considerations including error checking on memory allocation failures, network errors, and data inconsistencies, as well as ensuring compatibility across diverse environments. Error recovery mechanisms also need to be added based on the requirements.
']

