["Here is a simple example that demonstrates how to handle shared resources using semaphores in C. This code will not cover all your requirements because it's far beyond what can be fit into this model, but you should be able to adapt it or create a similar setup with the required functionality.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    int cell_id;
    double freq_band;
    double bandwidth;
    void** buffers; // Dynamically allocated array of buffer pointers
} LTEPHYCONTEXT_T;

sem_t semaphore;
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
LTEPHYCONTEXT_T* contexts; // Dynamically allocated array of context structures
int numContexts = 0;

void initializeAndConfigureContexts(int n) {
    sem_init(&semaphore, 0, 1); // Initialize the semaphore to value 1
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    contexts = (LTEPHYCONTEXT_T*) malloc(n * sizeof(LTEPHYCONTEXT_T));
    numContexts = n;

    // Initialize contexts here...

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

void processData() {
    sem_wait(&semaphore); // Wait for the semaphore before accessing shared resources

    // Process data with safe access to contexts and buffers

    sem_post(&semaphore); // Release the semaphore after completing processing
}

void cleanupAndDeallocate() {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    for(int i = 0; i < numContexts; ++i) {
        free(contexts[i].buffers); // Free dynamically allocated buffers
    }
    free(contexts); // Free dynamically allocated contexts array

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}
``` 

This code creates a semaphore and mutex to manage shared access to resources. It also initializes an array of context structures, which contain fields for cell identity, frequency band, and bandwidth configuration, as well as an array of pointers to dynamically allocated buffers. The `processData` function demonstrates how you would use the semaphore to safely access and process shared data, and the `cleanupAndDeallocate` function de-allocates all resources to avoid memory leaks.

