['Here's an example of how you could approach solving this problem in C using a combination of mutexes (for simple locking) and pthread_rwlock (for reader-writer locks). This code doesn't compile, it's just to give you a general idea of the concepts involved.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct Record {
    long offset;
    int key;
} Record;

Record* memory_pool;
int size = 10e6;  // assuming a million records, adjust as needed
pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
pthread_rwlock_t rwlock = PTHREAD_RWLOCK_INITIALIZER;

void* init_memory(int low, int high) {
    srand(time(0));
    memory_pool = malloc(sizeof(Record)*size);
    if (memory_pool == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed!\n");
        return;
    }

    for (int i=0; i<size; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);  // lock to avoid race conditions

        Record rec;
        rec.offset = ((long)rand() << 32) | rand();  // random offset
        rec.key = low + (rand() % (high - low + 1));  // random key within range

        memory_pool[i] = rec;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);  // unlock after modification
    }

    return memory_pool;
}

Record get_record(int index) {
    Record record;

    if (index >= size || index < 0) {
        printf("Index out of bounds!\n");
        return record;  // returns a default 'empty' record
    }

    pthread_rwlock_rdlock(&rwlock);  // acquire reader lock

    record = memory_pool[index];  // read from the pool

    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&rwlock);  // release lock
    return record;
}
```
This code initializes a large dynamic array of Record structures, each containing a random long offset and an integer key. It then provides functions to safely access these records. The use of mutexes ensures thread safety, while the use of reader-writer locks allows multiple threads to read from the memory pool concurrently without degrading performance or causing deadlocks, but only one thread can write at a time.

Please note that this is just a simplified example and doesn't include features like automatic resizing when memory pool is full, handling of memory allocation failures etc. A complete implementation would be much more complex. 

For best practices in managing memory, you should also consider using valgrind to identify memory leaks or possible segmentation faults in your code, for example. Also, it's always a good idea to profile and optimize the application before pushing to production environment.
